(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-membrane-potentials-action-potentials-quiz"]={"title":"Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials — Physiology Exam 1","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Why is the resting membrane potential of a typical neuron so close to the potassium equilibrium potential (EK)?","choices":["The membrane is far more permeable to Na+ than K+ at rest, due to roughly 100 times more Na+ leak channels than K+ leak channels being present","There are no leak channels at rest; the resting potential instead arises purely from the continuous activity of the Na+/K+ ATPase pump alone","Na+ and K+ permeability are equal at rest, causing the resting membrane potential to sit almost exactly midway between ENa and EK","The membrane is far more permeable to K+ than Na+ (about 100x more K+ leak channels)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The resting membrane potential is close to EK because the membrane is far more permeable to K+ than Na+ at rest, due to roughly 100x more K+ leak channels than Na+ leak channels. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why is the resting membrane potential of red blood cells (RBCs) so close to 0 mV, unlike most other cells?","choices":["RBCs have unusually few Cl-/HCO3- exchanger proteins compared to most other cell membranes","Resting membrane permeability to sodium ions is high compared to most other cell types","RBCs actively pump chloride out via an ATP-dependent transporter absent from most other cell types","RBCs have far fewer K+ leak channels relative to Na+ leak channels than most other cell types"],"answer":1,"exp":"The resting membrane potential of RBCs is close to 0 mV because their resting membrane permeability to sodium ions is high compared to most other cell types (unlike neurons, which are much more K+-permeable at rest). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How is the electrochemical driving force (VDF) on an ion calculated, and what does a positive VDF indicate?","choices":["VDF = Vm - Eion; a positive VDF means the ion enters the cell (influx)","VDF cannot be calculated for individual ions","VDF = Eion - Vm; a positive VDF always means no net movement","VDF = Vm - Eion; a positive VDF means the ion exits the cell (efflux)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The electrochemical driving force (VDF) for any ion equals Vm - Eion; a positive VDF means the ion exits the cell (efflux), while a negative VDF means the ion enters the cell (influx). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does an increase in extracellular potassium concentration affect cell excitability, and why?","choices":["It decreases excitability by hyperpolarizing the cell, since a higher extracellular K+ concentration pulls the resting potential further from threshold","It increases excitability because the resting membrane potential moves closer to the threshold potential","It has no measurable effect on excitability, since threshold potential shifts in the exact opposite direction to compensate for any change in resting potential","It only affects skeletal muscle excitability, since neurons regulate their resting potential independently of extracellular potassium concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"An increase in extracellular potassium concentration depolarizes the resting membrane potential, bringing it closer to the threshold potential and thus increasing cell excitability. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the effect of low plasma calcium on threshold potential and excitability?","choices":["Low calcium abolishes the action potential entirely by permanently inactivating voltage-gated sodium channels throughout the axon hillock","Threshold potential is completely unaffected by calcium concentration, changing instead only in response to shifts in extracellular potassium concentration","Threshold potential becomes more positive, moving further from resting Vm and decreasing excitability, which is why hypercalcemia can cause muscle weakness","Threshold potential becomes more negative (closer to resting Vm), increasing excitability"],"answer":3,"exp":"Low plasma calcium makes the threshold potential more negative, bringing it closer to the resting membrane potential and thus increasing cell excitability (this is why hypocalcemia can cause tetany/spontaneous firing). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which of the following is a defining property of the action potential?","choices":["It decays exponentially with distance from its point of origin, much like a graded electrotonic potential spreading passively along the membrane","It is an all-or-none event with constant amplitude that does not summate","It requires no voltage-gated channels at all, propagating instead purely through passive electrotonic spread of current along the axon membrane","It is a graded event whose amplitude varies continuously with stimulus strength, much like an excitatory postsynaptic potential at the synapse"],"answer":1,"exp":"The action potential is an all-or-none event (must reach threshold) with constant amplitude — it does not summate, and it propagates without decrement by relying on voltage-gated ion channels. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"According to the rule of thumb given, how is the conduction velocity of a myelinated fiber estimated from its diameter?","choices":["Velocity is unrelated to fiber diameter","Velocity (m/s) = square root of fiber diameter for myelinated fibers","Velocity (m/s) = fiber diameter (mm) x 4.5","Velocity (m/s) = diameter (mm) squared"],"answer":2,"exp":"For myelinated fibers, conduction velocity (m/s) is approximately fiber diameter (mm) x 4.5; for unmyelinated fibers, velocity is approximated by the square root of the fiber diameter. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why do local anesthetics typically produce analgesia (pain relief) without paralysis?","choices":["They preferentially block only large motor neurons, since motor axons have a much lower threshold for local anesthetic binding than the small unmyelinated sensory fibers carrying pain","They only work on heavily myelinated fibers, since local anesthetics require access to the node of Ranvier and cannot cross the continuous membrane of unmyelinated C-fibers","They are more effective against small-diameter neurons with a large surface-area-to-volume ratio, so small C-fibers (pain) are affected more than large motor neurons","They have no selective effect based on fiber size at all, blocking large motor neurons and small pain fibers at exactly the same drug concentration and rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Local anesthetics are more effective against small-diameter neurons with a large surface-area-to-volume ratio, so small C-fibers carrying pain sensation are blocked more readily than large alpha-motorneurons, producing analgesia without paralysis. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What happens to Na+ and K+ permeability during the upstroke of the action potential?","choices":["Both Na+ and K+ permeability decrease sharply as their voltage-gated channels close in direct response to the initial depolarizing stimulus itself","Only K+ permeability increases during the upstroke, opening K+ channels that drive the membrane potential rapidly and directly toward EK","Neither Na+ nor K+ permeability changes during the upstroke, which is instead generated entirely by passive capacitive current across the membrane","Na+ permeability increases (opening Na+ channels, moving Vm toward ENa); K+ permeability increase follows, moving Vm toward EK"],"answer":3,"exp":"During the upstroke of the action potential, Na+ permeability increases due to opening Na+ channels, moving the membrane potential toward ENa; K+ permeability then increases due to opening K+ channels, moving the potential toward EK. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What are the three main stimuli that cause ion channels to \"gate\" (transition between conducting and non-conducting states)?","choices":["Only ligand binding to a specific receptor protein, since voltage changes and mechanical forces alone cannot independently open any ion channel","Temperature, pH, and osmolarity changes, which together account for essentially all physiological gating of ion channels in excitable tissue","Changes in membrane potential (voltage-gated), occupation of a receptor (ligand-gated), and mechanical forces (mechanosensitive)","Only changes in membrane potential, since ligand binding and mechanical deformation cannot independently trigger channel gating in any tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ion channels gate in response to changes in membrane potential (voltage-gated channels), occupation of a receptor (ligand-gated/receptor-operated channels), or mechanical forces (mechanosensitive channels, important for hearing). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How do voltage-gated Na+ channels differ from K+ channels in terms of inactivation during depolarization?","choices":["K+ channels inactivate substantially faster than Na+ channels, closing within about a millisecond of depolarization and terminating the repolarizing current early","Both channel types show voltage inactivation during depolarization, entering a nonconducting state that must be reset by repolarization before reopening","Neither channel type shows activation in response to depolarization; both instead open passively whenever the membrane potential drifts near rest","Depolarization causes Na+ channels to both activate and inactivate; K+ channels show activation but not inactivation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Depolarization causes Na+ channels to activate (open) but also to inactivate (enter a nonconducting state); by contrast, K+ channels show activation but not inactivation — they simply deactivate (close) upon repolarization. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the difference between the absolute and relative refractory periods?","choices":["They are identical concepts, both describing the brief window immediately after an action potential during which Na+ channels remain fully available","Absolute refractory period requires a greater-than-normal stimulus due to partial Na+ channel inactivation; relative refractory period makes any new action potential completely impossible","Absolute refractory period: AP is impossible due to Na+ channel voltage inactivation; relative refractory period: a greater-than-normal stimulus is required","Refractory periods only apply to K+ channels, since Na+ channels reset instantly and can reopen immediately regardless of membrane potential"],"answer":2,"exp":"During the absolute refractory period, an action potential is impossible due to voltage inactivation of Na+ channels; during the relative refractory period, a stimulus greater than normal is required to elicit an action potential. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the effect of sphingomyelin in the myelin sheath on membrane capacitance and ion flow?","choices":["It decreases membrane capacitance and ion flow roughly 5000-fold","It only affects ion flow at the node of Ranvier, not elsewhere","It has no effect on capacitance, only slightly slowing ion flow","It increases membrane capacitance and ion flow substantially"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sphingomyelin in the myelin sheath (formed by Schwann cells wrapping the axon) decreases membrane capacitance and ion flow roughly 5000-fold, enabling faster, more energy-efficient saltatory conduction. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where are Na+ and K+ channels concentrated in a myelinated axon, enabling saltatory conduction?","choices":["Concentrated at the nodes of Ranvier only","Concentrated only at the axon hillock, not elsewhere","Concentrated only at the axon terminal, not elsewhere","Evenly distributed along the entire length of the axon"],"answer":0,"exp":"In myelinated axons, action potentials occur only at the nodes of Ranvier, where Na+ and K+ channels are concentrated — saltatory conduction (\"jumping\" node to node) increases AP velocity and conserves energy. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In a fast chemical synapse, how does a transmitter released from the presynaptic neuron affect the postsynaptic membrane?","choices":["It always causes hyperpolarization, regardless of receptor type","It only acts through slow second-messenger cascades, never directly","It directly opens ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic neuron","It has no direct electrical effect on the postsynaptic membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"In fast synapses, small-molecule transmitter substances released from the presynaptic neuron bind to receptors on the postsynaptic neuron, directly opening ligand-gated ion channels; the selectivity of these channels for particular ions determines whether the membrane depolarizes or hyperpolarizes. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How do graded (electrotonic) potentials differ from action potentials?","choices":["They have a brief refractory period identical to the action potential, during which a second graded potential cannot be generated at exactly the same membrane site","They are all-or-none events that propagate without decrement over long distances along the axon, exactly like the action potential that follows them","They involve no changes in ion permeability at all, arising instead from purely passive capacitive charging of the membrane at the site of stimulation","They are proportional to stimulus strength (graded), decay exponentially with distance, and can summate, unlike the all-or-none, non-summating, non-decremental AP"],"answer":3,"exp":"Graded (electrotonic) potentials are proportional to stimulus strength, are not propagated but instead decay exponentially with distance, exhibit summation, and have no refractory period — in contrast to the all-or-none, non-decremental, non-summating action potential. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the difference between temporal and spatial summation of excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs)?","choices":["Temporal summation involves successive EPSPs from the same synapse; spatial summation involves EPSPs from distant/different synapses that overlap","Temporal summation only occurs at inhibitory synapses, while spatial summation is a phenomenon unique to excitatory glutamatergic synapses","They are the same phenomenon, both describing successive EPSPs from a single synapse that summate because they arrive faster than the membrane can decay","Spatial summation requires no overlap in timing at all, only requiring that the summating EPSPs originate from anatomically adjacent dendrites"],"answer":0,"exp":"Temporal summation occurs when successive EPSPs from the same synapse summate because a new EPSP arrives before the previous one has fully decayed; spatial summation occurs when EPSPs from distant/different synapses overlap. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which ions are typically involved in generating an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP)?","choices":["Only Ca2+","K+ or Cl-","Na+ and Ca2+","Only Na+"],"answer":1,"exp":"An IPSP typically results from increased permeability to K+ or Cl-, which hyperpolarizes the postsynaptic cell and depresses excitability (as opposed to the cation-mediated EPSP). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the approximate equilibrium potential of the excitatory nicotinic ACh receptor/glutamate-type synapse, and what does it do to the postsynaptic cell?","choices":["~0 mV; depolarizes the postsynaptic cell (permeable to Na+, K+, and Ca2+), enhancing excitability","~ -90 mV; permeable mainly to Cl- ions, similar to GABA-A and glycine receptor channels, hyperpolarizing the postsynaptic cell and reducing its excitability","~ -70 mV; equal to the normal resting potential, so opening this channel produces no net current and has essentially no effect on excitability","~ +90 mV; permeable only to Ca2+, which always triggers an action potential directly at the postsynaptic membrane without requiring further summation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Excitatory synapses (e.g., nAChR, glutamate) are permeable to cations (Na+, K+, Ca2+) with a net equilibrium potential around 0 mV, depolarizing the postsynaptic cell and enhancing excitability — contrasted with inhibitory synapses (GABAA, glycine) permeable to Cl- with an equilibrium potential around -90 mV. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the motor end plate?","choices":["A specialized region of the presynaptic neuron only, containing the entire vesicle pool of acetylcholine ready for release into the synaptic cleft","A specialized junction structure found only in smooth muscle, where autonomic varicosities release transmitter diffusely across a wide synaptic gap","The site of concentrated Golgi apparatus within the muscle fiber, responsible for packaging newly synthesized membrane proteins near the sarcolemma","The specialized structure on the muscle fiber where the neuromuscular junction (synapse between motoneuron and muscle fiber) occurs, usually one per fiber"],"answer":3,"exp":"The neuromuscular junction is a specialized synapse between a motoneuron and a muscle fiber, occurring at a structure on the muscle fiber called the motor end plate — usually only one per fiber. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is found in the synaptic cleft of the neuromuscular junction in large quantities, and what is its function?","choices":["Acetylcholinesterase (AChE), which terminates the signal by breaking down ACh","Calmodulin, which binds Ca2+ to activate a postsynaptic kinase","Titin, which provides passive elasticity to the postsynaptic membrane","Troponin, which regulates postsynaptic sensitivity to acetylcholine"],"answer":0,"exp":"The synaptic cleft (20-30 nm wide) of the neuromuscular junction contains large quantities of acetylcholinesterase (AChE), which terminates ACh signaling; ACh-gated channels sit at the tops of the subneural clefts, while voltage-gated Na+ channels are in the bottom part. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What triggers the fusion of synaptic vesicles with the presynaptic membrane and release of ACh at the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Local depolarization opening voltage-gated Ca2+ channels at the nerve terminal, increasing cytosolic Ca2+, which triggers vesicle fusion and exocytosis","A rise in extracellular Na+ concentration alone, which is thought to draw synaptic vesicles toward the presynaptic membrane through a simple osmotic gradient effect","A drop in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration following the action potential, which is thought to relieve a resting inhibition on vesicle docking proteins and permit fusion","Direct binding of ACh to its own presynaptic autoreceptor, which triggers a positive feedback loop that further accelerates vesicle release"],"answer":0,"exp":"The action potential at the nerve terminal causes local depolarization that opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels; the resulting increase in cytosolic Ca2+ triggers fusion of roughly 125 synaptic vesicles with the presynaptic membrane, releasing ACh by exocytosis. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What are miniature end-plate potentials (MEPPs), and do they cause muscle contraction?","choices":["Potentials generated only in smooth muscle tissue, arising from spontaneous pacemaker activity of gap-junction-coupled cells rather than any vesicle fusion event","Full-blown action potentials generated spontaneously in the postsynaptic muscle fiber itself, entirely independent of any presynaptic acetylcholine release event","Large depolarizations caused by hundreds of vesicles fusing simultaneously in an uncontrolled burst; unlike a normal end-plate potential, they do cause contraction on their own","Small potentials caused by occasional spontaneous fusion of single ACh-filled vesicles with the presynaptic membrane; they do NOT cause muscle contraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"MEPPs arise when ACh-filled vesicles occasionally fuse spontaneously with the presynaptic membrane and release their contents, producing miniature end-plate potentials in the postsynaptic membrane — these do not cause muscle contraction on their own (unlike the summed effect of ~125 vesicles released by an action potential). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What ions does the ACh-gated (nicotinic) channel at the neuromuscular junction conduct, and what is its net equilibrium potential?","choices":["Na+, K+, and Ca2+ (a non-selective cation channel), with a net equilibrium potential of about 0 mV","Only Ca2+, functioning as a highly selective channel analogous to a voltage-gated L-type channel, with an equilibrium potential of about +60 mV","Only K+, functioning as a ligand-gated leak channel with an equilibrium potential around -90 mV, dampening rather than exciting the end plate","Only Cl-, functioning as an inhibitory ligand-gated channel with an equilibrium potential around -90 mV, similar to the GABA-A receptor"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ACh-gated channel is a non-selective cation channel that does not differentiate between Na+, K+, or Ca2+; each ion moves down its own electrochemical gradient, giving a net equilibrium potential of about 0 mV. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What terminates the end-plate potential at the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Reuptake of intact ACh into the presynaptic terminal","Closure of voltage-gated Ca2+ channels alone, with no ACh breakdown","Simple diffusion of ACh away from the synaptic cleft only","Acetylcholinesterase (AChE), which breaks down ACh"],"answer":3,"exp":"Acetylcholinesterase (AChE) breaks down ACh in the synaptic cleft, terminating the end-plate potential and allowing the neuromuscular junction to reset for the next signal. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is a ligand?","choices":["Only an endogenous substance made naturally by the body itself","A specialized subtype of voltage-gated ion channel","A structural scaffolding protein found within muscle","Any substance that binds to a receptor"],"answer":3,"exp":"A ligand is any substance that binds to a receptor; acetylcholine and norepinephrine are considered \"physiological\" ligands (provided by the body), and receptors are often classified/named by reference to their ligand. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What distinguishes nicotinic (nACh) from muscarinic (mACh) acetylcholine receptors?","choices":["Muscarinic receptors are found only within skeletal muscle at the neuromuscular junction, while nicotinic receptors are restricted entirely to smooth muscle organs","Nicotinic receptors are stimulated by nicotine; muscarinic receptors are stimulated by muscarine — both are subtypes of ACh receptor named by their distinguishing ligand","Nicotinic receptors do not respond to acetylcholine at all, being activated exclusively by nicotine itself, an entirely separate and unrelated ligand","They are functionally identical G protein-coupled receptors, differing only in the name given by the pharmacologist who first described them"],"answer":1,"exp":"ACh receptors are subdivided into nicotinic (nACh, stimulated by nicotine) and muscarinic (mACh, stimulated by muscarine) receptors, illustrating how receptors are often classified by reference to distinguishing ligands. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Approximately how many acetylcholine molecules are contained in a single synaptic vesicle at the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["~10,000","~100","~10","~1,000,000"],"answer":0,"exp":"Each synaptic vesicle at the neuromuscular junction contains approximately 10,000 molecules of ACh, with roughly 300,000 vesicles formed per nerve terminal. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How is information encoded by a series of action potentials, given that their amplitude is constant?","choices":["By varying the AP amplitude","By the frequency of action potentials","Information cannot be encoded by APs","By reversing the direction of propagation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because action potential amplitude is constant (they do not summate), information is encoded by the frequency of action potentials rather than by amplitude. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the typical threshold voltage for initiating an action potential, relative to resting potential?","choices":["About 15 mV positive to resting potential","About 50 mV positive to resting potential","About 100 mV negative to resting potential","Exactly equal to resting potential"],"answer":0,"exp":"Action potentials are typically initiated once the membrane depolarizes to about 15 mV positive to the resting potential (the threshold voltage), triggering the all-or-none upstroke. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 16"}]};
